# Layers and browser tests

### Where a component lives, and how a user interface is proved

Tutors Ladder, week 9. Rung 5 of 7: Reader. Upstream paths are at tutors-mono-repo `2b96b69`.

---

## Two questions for every component

1. **Where does it live?** In one of three UI layers, and the layers point one way.
2. **What proves it?** A scenario over the logic it shows, a browser test over the page it is on, or both.

The first half of this talk is the first question, here and upstream. The second half is the second.

---

## Three UI packages, one direction

Upstream `ARCHITECTURE.md`: "The one-directional dependency flow is: `ui-components` -> `ui-navigators` ->
`ui-primitives`."

| Package | Holds (`packages/svelte/...`) |
| --- | --- |
| `ui-primitives` | `Icon`, `Image`, `Menu`, `Sidebar`, `StudentCard`, `utils/sanitize.ts` |
| `ui-navigators` | `TutorsShell`, `MainNavigator`, `SecondaryNavigator`, `CourseNavigation`, `footers/`, `search/` |
| `ui-components` | `learning-objects/layout`, `structure`, `content`; `quiz/`, `time/` |

Below them: `runes`, `course`, `themes`, `utils/i18n`, `utils/a11y`.

---

## The rule that keeps it, upstream

`.dependency-cruiser.cjs`, proved able to fail by `tests/architecture/dependency-rules.test.ts`:

```js
name: "ui-primitives-no-data-features",
comment: "UI primitives are low-level presentational components: they take users, events and
  permissions as props or context, and never reach into the data and access packages
  (community, connect, data-api, rbac) themselves. Theme and tour services stay allowed.",
from: { path: "^packages/svelte/ui-primitives/" },
to:   { path: "^packages/svelte/(community|connect|data-api|utils/rbac)/" }
```

The same test proves `layer-ui-primitives`, `layer-ui-navigators` and `layer-ui-components` each catch a
fixture that reaches up.

---

## The same rule, at rung 5

`tests/architecture/reader-layers.test.ts`

```ts
"lib/course":        { may: [], kit: false, job: "... plain TypeScript, so Vitest runs it without a browser ..." },
"lib/runes":         { may: ["lib/course"], kit: false, ... },
"lib/ui-primitives": { may: [], kit: false, job: "... like upstream's ui-primitives they import no data ..." },
"lib/ui-navigators": { may: ["lib/ui-primitives", "lib/runes", "lib/course"], kit: true, ... },
"lib/ui-components": { may: ["lib/ui-navigators", "lib/ui-primitives", "lib/runes", "lib/course"], kit: true, ... },
routes:              { may: [all five], kit: true, job: "routes load a page's data and hand it to one component" },
```

Folders, not packages: a learner reads one app. The direction is upstream's. Each failure names the rule,
the file, the reason and the fix (L4).

---

## Where does a new piece go?

`AGENTS.md`, "Where things go":

| You are adding | It belongs in |
| --- | --- |
| a page | `src/routes/(course-reader)/<kind>/...` |
| finding things, deciding a 404 | `src/lib/course` |
| a card, a layout, a content view | `src/lib/ui-components` |
| breadcrumbs, a pager, search | `src/lib/ui-navigators` |
| a small piece with no data | `src/lib/ui-primitives` |
| shared state | `src/lib/runes` |

This table is standard work for you and for your agent. The assignment's pager is a navigator with its
logic in `lib/course`: two rows of the table.

---

## What upstream's layers carry that ours do not

| Concern | Upstream | Rung 5 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Themes | `packages/svelte/themes/src/services/themes.svelte.ts`: seven themes, a `data-theme` attribute, `localStorage.theme` | light and dark tokens in `app.css`; the fence forbids storage |
| Interface text | `packages/svelte/utils/i18n/src/index.ts`: `t(key)`, six locales (`en fr de it es ga`), `locale` is a rune | English in the markup |
| Accessibility | skip link and `main#main-content` in `TutorsShell`; focus after navigation; `aria-current="step"`; axe checks in `apps/reader/tests/e2e/accessibility.spec.ts` | the first four, in the same places; no axe |

Drift to notice: `ARCHITECTURE.md` lists five languages; the code has six, with Irish.

---

## What proves a user interface?

Rung 5 has five answers, cheapest first.

| Check | Sees | Blind to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `svelte-check` (L5) | wrong props, a missing colour, a lost `$derived` | behaviour |
| architecture rules (L4) | a layer reaching up, `{@html}`, a stray `fetch` | behaviour |
| scenarios and unit tests over `lib/course` (L3) | every rule you kept out of components | anything a component does |
| mutation (L3, outside the gate) | tests that would not notice a change | components: only `lib/course` is mutated |
| `pnpm test:e2e` (L3, outside the gate) | the page a student gets | why it is wrong |

No single row is enough. The art is putting each rule where the cheapest check can see it.

---

## The browser tier

`tests/e2e/serve.ts` serves `apps/reader/build` with a `200.html` fallback and launches Chromium.
`tests/e2e/smoke.e2e.ts`:

```ts
test("a learner clicks from the home page to a lab step (R-018, R-019, R-021)", async () => {
  await page.goto(reader.origin);
  await page.getByRole("link", { name: "Open the course" }).click();
  await page.getByRole("heading", { level: 1, name: "Ladder Sample Course" }).waitFor();
  ...
  await page.keyboard.press("ArrowRight");
  await page.getByRole("heading", { level: 1, name: "Build" }).waitFor();
  expect(errors).toEqual([]);
});
```

- It finds things as a person does: by role and name. That is also an accessibility check.
- Its title cites the requirements it exercises. The EARS audit checks those citations.
- It is the `playwright` library run by Vitest; upstream's `@playwright/test` runner is left to the Summit.

---

## What only the browser sees

Swap the arrow keys in `Lab.svelte` (`ArrowRight ? previous : ...`).

| Check | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `pnpm test` | green: 409 passed |
| `pnpm test:e2e` | red: timed out waiting for the heading "Build" |

R-019 ("link to the step before it and the step after it") is still true of `liveLab`. The component simply
used it the wrong way round.

This is a **defect that escapes**: every in-gate check green, the student's page wrong. In Lean terms it is
the most expensive defect, found by the customer. The browser tier is there to find it first.

---

## Why e2e is outside the gate

- It needs a build and a browser; the gate must run anywhere, quickly.
- So `AGENTS.md` makes it standard work: run `pnpm test:e2e` whenever you change what a learner sees.
- That is an L2 instruction about when to run an L3 check. It can be forgotten.

Where would you move it? Upstream runs its browser suite on every pull request (`ui-contract` in
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`). Rung 7 (Shipped) gives this ladder a CI to do the same.

---

## Upstream: Rules a browser proves

`guides/testing/EARS-METHODOLOGY.md`, "Browser-proved Rules". A feature tagged `@ui`:

```gherkin
@rule-0035 @ears-event-driven
Rule: When a student moves between lab steps by the pager, the step list, the arrow keys or browser
      history, the reader shall mark only the current step in the step list.

  Scenario: Step list follows the pager, links, keys and history
```

is proved by the Playwright test with **the same title**, tagged with the Rule id
(`apps/reader/tests/e2e/reading.spec.ts`):

```ts
test("Step list follows the pager, links, keys and history", { tag: "@rule-0035" }, async ({ page }) => {
```

`pnpm test:ears:audit` fails a scenario with no such test (`unproved-scenario`) and a test with no such
scenario (`orphan-ui-test`). Closure, again, across a new pair of lists.

---

## Two ways to specify a UI rule

| | Rung 5 | Upstream `@ui` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The Rule's scenario runs | in Node, over `lib/course` | in Chromium, over the page |
| The browser test | a smoke test that cites R-ids | one test per scenario, same title |
| Fast and precise when it fails | yes | less so |
| Sees layout, focus, colour, keys | no | yes |

Rung 5's choice fits a small reader with little that only a browser can see. The price is the row "What
only the browser sees". The second reading note works through when to choose which.

---

## Mutation: is the suite paying attention?

`pnpm test:mutation` changes `lib/course` over a hundred times and counts the changes no test noticed.

| File | Score | Floor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `course-service.ts` | 96.77 | 96 |
| `lo-tree.ts` | 100 | 100 |
| `live-lab.ts` | 100 | 100 |

The two survivors in `course-service.ts` are equivalent mutants; `RUNG.md` says why. Components are not
mutated: one more reason to keep rules out of them. A new file in `lib/course` needs a line in
`stryker.config.json` and a floor, or the gate fails. The assignment asks for both.

---

## What to take away

- Three UI layers, one direction, a rule with a reason; the same here and upstream.
- Put each rule where the cheapest check can see it: types, then architecture, then a scenario.
- A scenario over logic cannot see what a component does with it. A browser test can.
- Upstream binds `@ui` scenarios to Playwright tests by title and Rule id, and audits the binding.
- Value is what the student gets on the screen. Check there before you call it done.
